{! layout("base") !}
{! block("title") !}toke — A Language Built for AI{! end !}
{! block("content") !}

<section class="hero">
  <h1>A Language Built for <span>AI</span></h1>
  <p>A programming language purpose-built for LLM code generation. 40&ndash;75% fewer tokens. Faster inference. Lower cost.</p>
  <div class="cta">
    <a href="/docs" class="btn btn-primary">Get started</a>
    <a href="/docs/learn/01-why-toke" class="btn btn-outline">Why toke?</a>
    <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/toke" class="btn btn-outline">GitHub</a>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- The Problem                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h2>The Problem</h2>
</div>
<p class="section-desc">Every time an AI writes code, it generates one token at a time. Each token costs compute, time, and money. Today's programming languages were designed for humans &mdash; they're full of verbose keywords, boilerplate imports, and ceremony that LLMs must produce token by token.</p>
<p class="section-desc"><strong style="color:#ccc">toke</strong> is a programming language designed from first principles to minimise the number of tokens an LLM needs to generate. With a purpose-built tokenizer, toke uses 40&ndash;75% fewer tokens than Python, C, or Java for equivalent complete programs &mdash; and the gap widens as documentation standards increase, because toke's LLM training corpus is comment-stripped.</p>

<div class="explainer" style="max-width:860px;margin:1rem auto;padding-left:2rem;padding-right:2rem;">
  <p><strong>What is a token?</strong> LLMs don't read characters &mdash; they read <em>tokens</em>, which are chunks of text (roughly 3&ndash;4 characters each). The statement <code>return 0;</code> costs 4 tokens every time an LLM writes it. In toke, the same statement is <code>&lt;0;</code> &mdash; and with a purpose-built tokenizer trained on toke programs, the entire return-and-close pattern <code>&lt;0};</code> becomes a single token.</p>
</div>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- See the Difference                                                 -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->

<div class="section-heading">
  <h2>See the Difference</h2>
</div>

<div class="prompt-box">
  <span class="prompt-label">Prompt</span>
  <p>"Write a complete program that prints the 10th Fibonacci number"</p>
</div>

<p class="section-desc">toke is measured with its purpose-built BPE tokenizer. Other languages use cl100k_base (GPT-4) &mdash; the tokenizer their models actually use. Every example is a complete, runnable program.</p>

<div class="tv-tabs" style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 2rem;">
  <div class="tv-tab-bar">
    <button class="tv-tab-btn active" data-tab="see-toke">toke (~23 tokens)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="see-python">Python (41)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="see-c">C (59)</button>
    <button class="tv-tab-btn" data-tab="see-java">Java (62)</button>
  </div>

  <div class="tv-tab-panel active" id="see-toke">
    <div class="token-viz toke-p2">
      <pre class="tv-code"><code><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">m=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">fib;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">i=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">io:</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">std.io;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">f=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">fib(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">n:i64):i64{</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">if(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">n&lt;2){&lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">n}</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11">el{&lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">fib(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1">n-1)+</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">fib(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">n-2</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4">)};</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">f=</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6">main():i64{</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7">io.println(</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">fib(10</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">));</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10">&lt;0};</span></code></pre>
      <div class="tv-footer">
        <span class="token-badge toke-p2">~23 tokens &mdash; purpose-built toke tokenizer (estimated)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tv-note" style="margin:0.75rem 0;font-size:0.85rem;color:#888;line-height:1.4;">
      This is the complete program &mdash; module declaration, import, function, main, and I/O. Whitespace is non-structural in toke (semicolons terminate everything), so newlines and indentation are optional formatting that do not affect the token count. Token boundaries shown are estimated for the Phase 2 purpose-built BPE tokenizer, which will be trained on toke's default-syntax corpus. Common patterns like <code style="background:#161616;border:1px solid #222;border-radius:3px;padding:0.1em 0.3em;font-size:0.82em;color:#c792ea;">n:i64):i64{</code> and <code style="background:#161616;border:1px solid #222;border-radius:3px;padding:0.1em 0.3em;font-size:0.82em;color:#c792ea;">&lt;0};</code> become single tokens because they appear in virtually every program in the 46,730-program training corpus. The tokenizer is being finalised as part of Gate 2.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="tv-tab-panel" id="see-python">
    <div class="token-viz other">
      <pre class="tv-code"><code><span class="tv-tok tv-c0">def</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1"> fib</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">(n</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">:</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c4"> int</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c5">)</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c6"> -&gt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c7"> int</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c8">:&#x21B5;
</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c9">   </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c10"> if</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c11"> n</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c0"> &lt;</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c1"> </span><span class="tv-tok tv-c2">2</span><span class="tv-tok tv-c3">:&#x21B5;
